    "engine": "jupyter",
    "markdown": "---\ngd-head-tags:\n  - >-\n    `<script type=\"application/ld+json\">[{\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"SoftwareSourceCode\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\", \"description\":\n    \"High-performance graph analysis library\", \"codeRepository\":\n    \"https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase\", \"license\":\n    \"https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT\", \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"documentation\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"programmingLanguage\": {\"@type\":\n    \"ComputerLanguage\", \"name\": \"Python\"}}, {\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"WebPage\", \"name\": \"Graph.check_planarity - EdgeCase\", \"isPartOf\":\n    {\"@type\": \"WebSite\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\"}, \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase/reference/Graph.check_planarity.html\"}]</script>`{=html}\nopen-graph:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.check_planarity()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: >-\n    Test planarity and return the evidence: an embedding or a Kuratowski\n    subgraph.\ntwitter-card:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.check_planarity()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: >-\n    Test planarity and return the evidence: an embedding or a Kuratowski\n    subgraph.\ntitle: >-\n  [Graph.check_planarity()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n  .doc-label-method}\nbody-classes: doc-api-page doc-py-api-page\nshift-heading-level-by: 0\nbread-crumbs: false\ntoc-depth: 3\ndescription-meta: >-\n  Test planarity and return the evidence: an embedding or a Kuratowski subgraph.\npage-navigation: false\nhtml-table-processing: none\n---\n\n::: {.doc-subject}\n\nTest planarity and return the evidence: an embedding or a Kuratowski subgraph.\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-usage-source}\nUsage\n\n[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L25576-L25693){target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"}\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}\n```pythonsignature\nGraph.check_planarity(counterexample=False)\n```\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-text}\n\n![Check planarity](../assets/graphs/check-planarity.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt=\"Left: a wheel with hub 1 and rim 2 to 6, drawn so that going clockwise around the hub meets 2, 6, 5, 4, 3, which is the hub's entry in the embedding that check_planarity() returns with True. Right: the Petersen graph, which is not planar; check_planarity(True) returns False with a counterexample h, shown in the accent color: the graph without node 1 (dashed), a subdivided K3,3 whose six branch nodes are 3, 9, 10 and 4, 7, 8.\" width=\"100%\"}\n\nRuns the same exact test as `is_planar()`, but also returns its certificate. For a\nplanar graph that is a planar embedding (a rotation system): for every node, its\nneighbors in clockwise order around it in some drawing without edge crossings. Any\nstraight-line or curved drawing that keeps these cyclic orders around every node can be\nmade crossing-free; `layout_planar()` computes one. For a non-planar graph, with\n`counterexample=True`, it is a Kuratowski subgraph: a subdivision of `K_5` or `K_{3,3}`,\nwhich by Kuratowski's theorem is what every non-planar graph contains.\n\nThis follows NetworkX's `check_planarity()`. Edge direction, self-loops and parallel\nedges are ignored, so each neighbor appears once in the embedding. NetworkX returns a\n`PlanarEmbedding` object; here the embedding is a plain dictionary of the same clockwise\nneighbor orders (`PlanarEmbedding.neighbors_cw_order()` in NetworkX). A graph usually has\nmany planar embeddings, and the one returned here need not be the one NetworkX picks.\n:::\n\n## Parameters {.doc-parameters}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code>[counterexample]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [bool]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [[False]{.va}]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>\n\n:   If `True`, return a Kuratowski subgraph when the graph is not planar. Finding it\n    reruns the planarity test once per edge, so it costs `O(e^2)` time; with `False`\n    (default), `None` is returned instead.\n:::\n\n## Returns {.doc-returns}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [tuple[bool, Optional[Union[dict[int, list[int]], Graph]]]]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   `(True, embedding)` if the graph is planar, where `embedding` maps each node ID to\n    its neighbors' IDs in clockwise order (an empty list for an isolated node).\n    `(False, None)` if it is not, or `(False, subgraph)` with `counterexample=True`:\n    a new graph holding the Kuratowski subgraph's edges (for a pair of nodes joined by\n    parallel edges, the one with the lowest ID) and their endpoints, with their IDs and\n    attributes.\n:::\n\n## See Also {.doc-see-also}\n\n[](`~is_planar`) \n\n:   Just the yes-or-no answer.\n\n[](`~layout_planar`) \n\n:   Positions that draw a planar graph without edge crossings.\n\n## Technical Notes {.doc-technical-notes}\n\nThe embedding comes from the left-right planarity test, which assigns each back edge\nof a depth-first search to the left or right side of the tree and then orders the edges\naround each node by their nesting depth. For a connected graph, tracing the faces of the\nresult gives the `e - n + 2` faces that Euler's formula requires of a planar drawing.\n\nThe counterexample is found as NetworkX does: drop each edge in turn and put it back\nwhenever the rest becomes planar. What remains is non-planar, but removing any one of\nits edges makes it planar, so it is a minimal non-planar subgraph, which can only be a\nsubdivision of `K_5` or `K_{3,3}`.\n\n## Examples {.doc-examples}\n\nIn a planar embedding of a wheel, the neighbors of the hub (node `1`) come around it in the\norder of the rim, `2` to `6`:\n\n::: {#527195b5 .cell execution_count=1}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nimport edgecase as ec\n\nwheel = ec.Graph().add_wheel_graph(6)\n\nplanar, embedding = wheel.check_planarity()\nplanar, embedding[1]\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}\n```\n(True, [2, 6, 5, 4, 3])\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n### Roads that have to cross\n\nTwelve towns are joined by 26 roads. They can't all be drawn without two of them crossing,\nand the counterexample shows why: 8 of the towns and 11 of the roads form `K_{3,3}`, with\ntwo of its edges stretched through Halston and Larkhill:\n\n::: {#69d4a7b3 .cell execution_count=2}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\ntowns = ec.towns()\n\nplanar, kuratowski = towns.check_planarity(counterexample=True)\nlabels = {\n    n: kuratowski.get_label_from_id(n) for n in kuratowski.get_node_ids()\n}\ndegrees = kuratowski.degrees(return_dict=True)\n\n(\n    planar,\n    kuratowski.count_nodes(),\n    kuratowski.count_edges(),\n    sorted(labels[n] for n, d in degrees.items() if d == 2),\n)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}\n```\n(False, 8, 11, ['Halston', 'Larkhill'])\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\nOn the map, the counterexample is highlighted. Here the road from Elmwick to Greyford\ncrosses the one from Calder to Fenmoor, and however the towns are placed, one of these roads\nwould have to bridge another:\n\n::: {#c30f6077 .cell execution_count=3}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\ntowns.plot(\n    layout=\"pos\",\n    highlight=list(labels.values()),\n    highlight_edges=[\n        (labels[s], labels[t])\n        for _, s, t in kuratowski.get_edges_as_tuples()\n    ],\n    label_by=\"label\",\n)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=3}\n```{=html}\n<div class=\"ec-theme\" style=\"display:contents\"><style class=\"ec-theme-vars\">:where(.ec-theme){--ect-3239c383:#fafafa;--ect-e5dfd507:#94a3b8;--ect-8a2de1d4:#374151;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(15,23,42,0.88);--ect-21ade003:#f1f5f9;--ect-61169ea5:#c2410c;--ect-63cc1385:#d4d4d8;--ect-879b7ebc:#949497;--ect-b9f8de6d:#872d08;--ect-f59ba8df:#a1a1aa}@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark){.e